  "slug": "quantum-circuit-compilation-0208",
  "summary": "New stack and workflow papers appear alongside end-to-end estimates that test chemistry and ML advantage claims against classical methods and fault-tolerant targets.",
  "synthesis": "The current items shift from hardware-aware compilation per se to the surrounding software and cost-accounting layer. Cqlib offers a Rust-core stack with Python and C interfaces spanning simulation, compilation, algorithms, and QCIS hardware, while TyxonQ links QM/MM, fragment embedding, classical CCSD/FCI, and quantum solvers to MD engines for incremental quantum integration. The DOE blueprint sets a fault-tolerant target of 100+ logical qubits by 2028, scaling to 1,000-10,000. Two estimation papers temper advantage expectations: quantum linear solver training for linearized neural networks crosses over classically only at very large sizes, and SHCI/TrimCI benchmarks on 26 chemistry instances find classical computation cheaper for six of seven systems. The common thread is evaluating quantum workflows by end-to-end runtime and cost rather than abstract algorithmic scaling.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
